            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"Frankenstein\" and Its Success</strong><br>\nMary Shelley's most famous work, \"Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus,\" was published in 1818. The novel, which she began writing at 18, was an immediate success and is often regarded as the first true science fiction story.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Career and Widowhood</strong><br>\nAfter Percy Shelley's death in 1822, Mary focused on her writing to support herself and her son. She wrote several other novels, including \"The Last Man\" and \"Lodore,\" and worked to edit and promote her late husband's poetry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Travels and Later Life</strong><br>\nMary's life was marked by travel throughout Europe, which influenced her writing. She spent time in Italy, France, and Switzerland, where she absorbed different cultural influences and historical perspectives.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
