          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Challenges and Illness</strong><br>\nMary Shelley faced many challenges, including the death of three of her four children and the struggle with illness. Her resilience in the face of such adversity is reflected in her personal letters and her fiction.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Views on Feminism and Society</strong><br>\nAlthough less overtly political than her mother or husband, Mary's writings often subtly critique the social roles available to women and the struggles they face, echoing her mother's feminist philosophies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Impact</strong><br>\nMary Shelley died in 1851, leaving behind a legacy that extended beyond \"Frankenstein.\" Her works contribute to discussions on the role of science, the nature of humanity, and the structure of society, ensuring her place as a pivotal figure in literary and intellectual history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
